  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nConditions remain blustery this afternoon with the chilly \ntemperatures and northwest winds gusting 25-35 mph, but drier \nair moving will at least allow for more sunshine as the \nafternoon goes on. Winds will diminish toward sunset as we lose \nmixing and the pressure gradient relaxes with high pressure \nbuilding overhead tonight. This sets up a good night for \nradiational cooling with latest dewpoint observations serving as\na pretty good proxy for tonight's lows...with most seeing lows \nin the 20s, and some of the northern valleys could see lows in \nthe teens, especially if some lower clouds hold off until closer\nto daybreak. The forecast lows are closer to the colder MET and\nMAV solutions. \n\nDry air and high pressure stay overhead Sunday, bringing a mostly \nsunny day. Temperatures will recover slightly into the low-mid 50s \nsouth of the mountains, but it will feel more comfortable than today \nas winds will be much lighter. Sunday night doesn't look quite as \ncold with forecast lows in the 20s and 30s. \n\nMonday and Monday night will remain dry with high pressure still in \ncontrol but may start to feature some more high clouds stemming from \nthe nor'easter off the coast of the Carolinas. Winds will again \nremain fairly light with highs reaching the mid 50s to low 60s. \n\nHigh pressure begins to push the meandering nor'easter back to \nthe south during the day Tuesday which is going to bring about \nclearing skies, and probably the warmest day of the week with \nwidespread temperatures topping out in the low 60s. Seasonable \ntemperatures and dry conditions then continue as high pressure \ncrests over the region again Wednesday and lasts through at \nleast the day Thursday. Late week a strong ridge building over \nthe central U.S. moves eastward and finally kicks the nor'easter\nout of its stall and off to the northeast. Virtually all model \nsolutions keep it well out so sea, but this could mean we see \nsome increased cloud coverage in the Friday-Saturday timeframe \nas it passes by. Confidence remains high that we stay dry as \nthat ridge eventually settles overhead prolonging what is \nalready an extended stretch of benign weather.",
